  "env_text": "You are in the XLand MiniGrid world, a grid-based puzzle level. This level uses layout R1: a single rectangular room (no interior walls). The map is 15x15, and you have up to 675 steps. The agent sees an egocentric 5x5 symbolic window (partially observable, not pixels). The agent position comes from ctx.get(\"agent_pos\", jnp.array([-1, -1], dtype=jnp.int32)). Observation interface (symbolic-first): use ctx.get(\"visible_object_positions\", {}) for currently visible objects and ctx.get(\"visible_object_positions_prev\", {}) for the previous step. Each lookup returns local [row, col] view coordinates, and [-1, -1] means not visible. Example visible-object lookup: ctx.get(\"visible_object_positions\", {}).get(\"blue_key\", jnp.array([-1, -1], dtype=jnp.int32)). Raw symbolic observation fallback: obs = ctx.get(\"observation\", ts_next.observation).astype(jnp.int32) and obs_prev = ctx.get(\"observation_prev\", ts_prev.observation).astype(jnp.int32). obs[..., 0] is tile id and obs[..., 1] is color id. Useful ids for observation-based checks: tiles FLOOR=1, WALL=2, BALL=3, SQUARE=4, PYRAMID=5, GOAL=6, KEY=7, HEX=11, STAR=12; colors RED=1, GREEN=2, BLUE=3, PURPLE=4, YELLOW=5, GREY=6, BLACK=7. Available actions are move_forward, turn_left, turn_right, pick_up, put_down, and toggle; the agent can carry only one object at a time (check ctx.get(\"is_carrying\", jnp.array(False)) and ctx.get(\"carried_item\", jnp.array([-1, -1], dtype=jnp.int32))). Initial objects include: blue key, red star, pink square, purple goal, yellow hexagon. Object locations come from ctx.get(\"object_positions\", {}).get(\"blue_key\", jnp.array([-1, -1], dtype=jnp.int32)). To get positions for the other initial objects, swap the key to one of: \"red_star\", \"pink_square\", \"purple_goal\", \"yellow_hexagon\". Your task is to place the blue key immediately down of the red star. Success when the blue key is exactly one cell down of the red star (same column, adjacent rows); use ctx.get(\"object_positions\", {}).get(\"blue_key\", jnp.array([-1, -1], dtype=jnp.int32)) and ctx.get(\"object_positions\", {}).get(\"red_star\", jnp.array([-1, -1], dtype=jnp.int32)) to locate the tiles. Use distances and spatial relations; avoid Python-side branching.",
